<Project>
  <!--
    CENTRAL PACKAGE MANAGEMENT (CPM)
    ================================
    Every NuGet version in this solution is declared exactly once, here.
    Individual .csproj files then reference packages WITHOUT a Version attribute:

        <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation" />

    Why enterprises do this:
      * One place to bump a version -> no "project A is on 8.0.1, project B on 8.0.4" drift.
      * Security patching becomes a one-line diff instead of a 30-file sweep.
      * A rogue transitive version can be pinned globally.

    Covered in: course/module-00-setup/03-build-system.md
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>true</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <!-- ── Persistence ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="10.0.10" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design" Version="10.0.10" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational" Version="10.0.10" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer" Version="10.0.10" />
    <!--
      SQLite is here for ONE project: LogiFlow.Academy.Api, the accounts service
      behind site/. That service has to run for someone who has just cloned the
      repository and has no SQL Server and no Docker, so its default provider is
      a file on disk. It can be pointed at SQL Server with one configuration key
      (Academy:Provider), which is the whole argument for provider abstraction
      made concrete. Nothing else in the solution uses it.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite" Version="10.0.10" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Data.SqlClient" Version="6.1.4" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions" Version="10.0.10" />
    <!--
      The full generic host, for LogiFlow.Wcs. A warehouse control system is a worker rather
      than a web application: it has no HTTP surface, and it must keep dispatching when nobody
      is looking at a browser. Hosting.Abstractions above is the interface-only slice the other
      libraries reference; this is the implementation, needed only by the process that hosts it.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting" Version="10.0.10" />
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Instrumentation.Runtime" Version="1.17.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Serilog.Sinks.OpenTelemetry" Version="4.2.0" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <!-- ── Application / cross-cutting ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="FluentValidation" Version="12.1.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="FluentValidation.DependencyInjectionExtensions" Version="12.1.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="10.0.10" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Abstractions" Version="10.0.10" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.StackExchangeRedis" Version="10.0.10" />
    <!--
      The SignalR Redis BACKPLANE. Note it is a different package from the cache above, and
      solves a different problem with the same server: the cache shares DATA between instances,
      the backplane shares MESSAGES. An application can want either, both, or neither.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.SignalR.StackExchangeRedis" Version="10.0.10" />

    <!--
      gRPC. Grpc.AspNetCore is a metapackage: it brings the server, Google.Protobuf, and
      Grpc.Tools, which is the one that matters most and is the least obvious. Grpc.Tools is a
      BUILD-TIME package - it runs protoc during compilation and generates C# from every
      <Protobuf> item in the csproj. That is why nothing in Grpc/ has a corresponding
      OrderReply.cs to read: it does not exist until you build.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Grpc.AspNetCore" Version="2.76.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Grpc.Net.Client" Version="2.76.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="10.0.10" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options.ConfigurationExtensions" Version="10.0.10" />
    <!--
      ValidateDataAnnotations() lives here and NOT in Microsoft.Extensions.Options, which
      surprises everyone the first time: the [Required] and [Range] attributes on an options
      class do nothing at all until this package is referenced, and the call that would have
      applied them does not compile. ASP.NET Core apps get it transitively from the shared
      framework, so the gap only appears in a class library - exactly where the options class
      usually lives.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options.DataAnnotations" Version="10.0.10" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience" Version="10.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <!-- ── Mail ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <!--
    MailKit, and NOT System.Net.Mail.SmtpClient.

    The framework's SmtpClient still compiles, and its own documentation has told
    you not to use it for years: it predates async properly, cannot do modern
    STARTTLS negotiation cleanly, and leaks connections if you dispose it wrong.
    Microsoft's remarks section names MailKit as the recommended replacement -
    which is a rare thing for a framework to do, and worth knowing when someone
    asks you in an interview why you took a dependency instead of using the BCL.

    MimeKit arrives with it and is the half that actually matters: it builds the
    RFC 5322 message, does the header encoding, and is what makes the .eml files
    the development transport writes openable in a real mail client.

    Covered in: course/module-10-cross-cutting/05-mailing.md
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="MailKit" Version="4.17.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="MimeKit" Version="4.17.0" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <!-- ── Web / API ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi" Version="10.0.10" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer" Version="10.0.10" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Scalar.AspNetCore" Version="2.16.18" />
    <PackageVersion Include="AspNetCore.HealthChecks.SqlServer" Version="9.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <!-- ── Security overrides for TRANSITIVE dependencies ──────────────────── -->
  <!--
    A REAL EXAMPLE, NOT A HYPOTHETICAL.

    Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi 10.0.10 declares a dependency on
    Microsoft.OpenApi >= 2.0.0. Version 2.0.0 carries a known HIGH severity
    advisory: GHSA-v5pm-xwqc-g5wc, which is the same advisory as CVE-2026-49451.
    Both ids are given deliberately. NuGet and the GitHub advisory database use
    the GHSA one, most other tooling uses the CVE, and quoting only one of them
    leaves the next reader believing there are two separate problems.

    We never reference that package directly - it arrives transitively - so
    there is no .csproj line to edit.

    Two things make the fix possible:
      1. NuGetAudit (on by default since .NET 8) reported it as NU1903, and
         because src/Directory.Build.props sets TreatWarningsAsErrors, the
         RESTORE FAILED instead of quietly shipping a vulnerable dependency.
      2. CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled (top of this file) lets a
         PackageVersion entry override a version nobody asked for directly.

    CHOOSING THE RIGHT PATCHED VERSION - the part that takes judgement.

    The advisory lists TWO patched lines: 2.7.5+ and 3.5.4+. Jumping to the
    newest (3.9.0) looks obviously best and breaks the build: Microsoft.OpenApi
    3.x made IOpenApiMediaType.Example read-only, and the OpenAPI XML-comment
    source generator shipped inside Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi 10.0.10 still
    assigns to it. The result is CS0200 in generated code you cannot edit.

    So we take the patched version on the SAME major line the framework was
    built against. Newest is not the goal; patched and compatible is.

    Read the advisory before picking a version. This one is availability-only
    (stack overflow on parsing a crafted document), which is worth knowing when
    you have to justify a same-day patch versus a scheduled one.
    Covered in: course/module-13-deployment/03-supply-chain.md — case one
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.OpenApi" Version="2.7.6" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <!--
    A SECOND REAL ONE, found the same way.

    Adding Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite 10.0.10 failed the RESTORE:

        error NU1903: Package 'SQLitePCLRaw.lib.e_sqlite3' 2.1.11 has a known
        high severity vulnerability, GHSA-2m69-gcr7-jv3q

    That is CVE-2025-6965 - a memory-corruption flaw in SQLite itself, fixed
    upstream in SQLite 3.50.2. The advisory listed no patched NuGet version at
    the time it was published, which is the situation that tempts people to add
    <NoWarn>NU1903</NoWarn> and move on. Don't: that silences the check for
    every future advisory too, not just this one.

    The package family has since shipped 2.1.12 and 2.1.13 carrying a newer
    embedded SQLite, so the fix is to pin the whole family forward. It has to be
    the WHOLE family - bundle, core, provider and lib are versioned in lockstep,
    and pinning only the flagged one produces a bundle loading a native library
    it was not built against.

    None of these is referenced directly by any project here; they all arrive
    transitively under EF Core's SQLite provider, which is why
    CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled (top of this file) is what makes the
    override possible at all.

    Covered in: course/module-13-deployment/03-supply-chain.md — case two
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlite3" Version="2.1.13" />
    <PackageVersion Include="SQLitePCLRaw.core" Version="2.1.13" />
    <PackageVersion Include="SQLitePCLRaw.lib.e_sqlite3" Version="2.1.13" />
    <PackageVersion Include="SQLitePCLRaw.provider.e_sqlite3" Version="2.1.13" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <!-- ── Observability ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Serilog.AspNetCore" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Extensions.Hosting" Version="1.17.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Instrumentation.AspNetCore" Version="1.17.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Instrumentation.Http" Version="1.17.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Instrumentation.SqlClient" Version="1.13.0-beta.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Exporter.OpenTelemetryProtocol" Version="1.17.0" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <!-- ── Testing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.1.4" />
    <PackageVersion Include="coverlet.collector" Version="6.0.4" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Shouldly" Version="4.3.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="NSubstitute" Version="6.1.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="NetArchTest.Rules" Version="1.3.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing" Version="10.0.10" />
    <!-- The SignalR CLIENT, used by the tests to connect to the hub the way a browser would.
         A hub tested through a mocked IHubContext proves the mapping and nothing else; the
         interesting failures (auth on the handshake, group routing, serialization) all live
         in the part a mock replaces. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.SignalR.Client" Version="10.0.10" />
    <!--
      Testcontainers.MsSql WAS here, and its removal is worth a note rather than a silent
      deletion.

      The integration tests used it to start a throwaway SQL Server container per run. When the
      repository moved off Docker they were retargeted at a locally installed SQL Server with a
      per-run database instead (see tests/LogiFlow.Api.IntegrationTests/LogiFlowApiFactory.cs),
      and the package became dead weight.

      Dead weight with a CVE attached, as it happens. It pulled in SSH.NET 2025.1.0
      transitively - Testcontainers uses it for SSH port forwarding - and that version carries
      GHSA-q939-rpr3-3284, a high severity advisory. So `dotnet build` on this solution emitted
      NU1903 on every single run.

      The lesson is the one this file keeps making: the cheapest way to fix a vulnerable
      dependency is to stop depending on it. A pin would have worked too, but pinning a package
      nothing uses is maintenance you have volunteered for permanently.

      Covered in: course/module-13-deployment/03-supply-chain.md - case three
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="BenchmarkDotNet" Version="0.15.8" />

    <!--
      NBomber: load testing, in C#. The counterpart to BenchmarkDotNet above and the answer to
      a different question - BDN measures a method in isolation, NBomber measures the system
      under concurrent load, where connection pools, lock contention and optimistic-concurrency
      retries live. Neither substitutes for the other.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="NBomber" Version="6.1.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="NBomber.Http" Version="6.0.0" />

    <!--
      A TRANSITIVE CVE, and the fix, left visible on purpose.

      NBomber 6.1.2 resolves MessagePack 2.5.192, which carries GHSA-vh6j-jc39-fggf (high) plus
      two moderates. NuGetAudit reported all three on the first restore. The package we asked
      for is fine; the package IT asked for is not, which is the case central package management
      exists to give you a lever over.

      The lever: a PackageVersion plus a direct PackageReference pins the transitive resolution,
      so one line moves MessagePack for every project that ends up with it.

      Module 13 golden rule 5 says "newest is not the goal, patched and compatible is", and the
      first attempt here followed it exactly - move to 2.5.198, the newest patch on the line
      NBomber was built against, and stay off a major version. It did not work: 2.5.198 still
      carries GHSA-vh6j-jc39-fggf, and so does 3.1.4. The advisory is only cleared in 3.1.8.

      So the rule met the case it does not cover: sometimes there IS no patched version on your
      line, and the choice is a major-version jump or a known-vulnerable dependency. The jump
      wins - but it is a jump, it is why this comment exists, and it is why the load tests are
      excluded from LogiFlow.slnf. If NBomber ever breaks against MessagePack 3.x, the failure
      will be here, and this paragraph is the note explaining what was traded for what.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="MessagePack" Version="3.1.8" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>